# Messages [9 messages]: [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Llumnix: Dynamic Scheduling for Large Language Model Serving  \nAuthors: Biao Sun, Ziming Huang, Hanyu Zhao, Wencong Xiao, Xinyi Zhang, Yong Li, Wei Lin (Alibaba Group)\n\nAbstract  \nLlumnix is a dynamic scheduling system for LLM inference that supports request rescheduling across multiple model instances. It achieves better load balancing, reduces memory fragmentation, enforces request prioritization, and supports scalable auto-scaling. It introduces near-zero-downtime live migration of requests and outperforms INFaaS++ by up to 15× in latency and achieves up to 36% cost savings.\n\n1. Introduction  \nLLM inference workloads are heterogeneous and unpredictable. Llumnix proposes request rescheduling (like OS context switching) across model instances to balance load, mitigate fragmentation, and prioritize requests. It unifies multiple scheduling goals under a live migration mechanism.\n\n2. Background  \nDiscusses LLM serving features like autoregressive generation, batching with dynamic memory (PagedAttention), and common challenges: latency sensitivity, unpredictability, and high memory demand. Highlights inefficiencies of static dispatch and scheduling.\n\n3. Motivation  \nEmpirical observations show:  \n- Request preemptions are costly (up to 70% of P99 latency).  \n- Performance interference across requests exists due to compute/memory contention.  \n- Fragmentation causes queuing despite available total memory.  \n- Priorities are not respected in current systems.  \nLlumnix proposes request migration to overcome these issues.\n\n4. Llumnix Design  \n- Live Migration: Overlaps KV cache copying with computation using a staged pipeline, minimizing downtime.  \n- Distributed Architecture: Global scheduler dispatches at instance level; instance-local “llumlets” handle request-level decisions and migration.  \n- Virtual Usage: Abstracts physical memory + scheduling context to guide balancing, prioritization, and de-fragmentation.  \n- Policy: Uses “freeness” (available memory scaled by batch size) to make decisions for dispatching, migration, and auto-scaling.\n\n5. Implementation  \nLlumnix integrates with vLLM using 3,300 lines of Python and Ray actors. Uses Gloo for KV cache transfers, with pipelining to minimize blocking. Block fusion improves data transfer. Offers fault tolerance and graceful fallback mechanisms.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- Migration: Near-zero overhead (20–30ms) vs. 3.5s for recompute.  \n- Performance: 15× lower P99 latency vs. INFaaS++; 84% reduction in preemption loss.  \n- Priorities: High-priority requests see 1.5× faster latency; normal requests are largely unaffected.  \n- Auto-scaling: Up to 36% cost saving with equivalent latency using smarter scaling decisions.  \n- Scalability: Llumnix supports 64-instance clusters with near-zero scheduling stalls.\n\n7. Related Work  \nCovers prior works like vLLM, Orca, INFaaS, TritonServer, etc., emphasizing that they target single-instance or stateless models. Llumnix is unique in enabling live migration and multi-instance dynamic coordination.\n\n8. Conclusion  \nLlumnix provides a unified architecture for LLM inference serving, inspired by OS-level abstractions. Through migration and dynamic scheduling, it balances performance, isolation, and cost in multi-tenant GPU clusters.\n\nAppendix  \nArtifact and scripts are available at [https://github.com/AlibabaPAI/llumnix](https://github.com/AlibabaPAI/llumnix) for reproduction of results.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Got it!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Where can the reproduction artifacts for Llumnix be found?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "The metadata carried across elements in Click's design is typically carried in annotations or attributes that are attached to packets or other data structures. These annotations or metadata can carry information such as destination IP, TTL, or other relevant data that is used by different elements to make decisions or adjust the processing of packets."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How does block fusion improve data transfer efficiency?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "The potential for Click's modular router design to achieve efficient and resilient operations is demonstrated in several ways:"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How does Llumnix compare to INFaaS++ in P99 latency reduction?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'The performance of Llumnix compared to INFaaS++ in P99 latency reduction is not specified in the given text.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What problem does Llumnix solve in large language model serving?'}]
